(window.LEARN_C = window.LEARN_C || {})["mth-12-03"] = {"3.1":{"s":"A trigonometric equation holds only for certain angles. Because sine, cosine and tangent repeat, one solution gives infinitely many; the formula that lists them all, using an integer n, is the general solution. The principal solutions are those between 0 and 2π. Standard results cover sin, cos and tan of θ equal to those of a known angle α, and their squares.","f":[{"x":"sin(theta) = sin(alpha) -> theta = n*pi + (-1)^n*alpha","n":"General solution; n is any integer.","r":"Balbharati Maths XII Part 1, p.68"},{"x":"cos(theta) = cos(alpha) -> theta = 2*n*pi +- alpha","n":"General solution of the cosine equation.","r":"Balbharati Maths XII Part 1, p.68"},{"x":"tan(theta) = tan(alpha) -> theta = n*pi + alpha","n":"General solution of the tangent equation.","r":"Balbharati Maths XII Part 1, p.68"},{"x":"sin^2(theta) = sin^2(alpha) -> theta = n*pi +- alpha","n":"Same form for cos^2 and tan^2.","r":"Balbharati Maths XII Part 1, p.69"}],"w":["sin θ = 0 gives θ = nπ, but cos θ = 0 gives θ = (2n + 1)π/2.","Do not cancel a factor like sin θ from both sides: you lose the solutions where it is zero."]},"3.2":{"s":"Solving a triangle means finding its unknown sides and angles from the known ones (at least one must be a side). In triangle ABC the sides opposite A, B, C are a, b, c. The sine rule, cosine rule and projection rule link them. From these come the half-angle formulas in terms of the semi-perimeter s, Heron's formula for the area, and Napier's analogy.","f":[{"x":"a/sin(A) = b/sin(B) = c/sin(C) = 2*R","n":"Sine rule; R = circumradius.","r":"Balbharati Maths XII Part 1, p.77"},{"x":"a^2 = b^2 + c^2 - 2*b*c*cos(A)","n":"Cosine rule (likewise for b^2 and c^2).","r":"Balbharati Maths XII Part 1, p.79"},{"x":"a = b*cos(C) + c*cos(B)","n":"Projection rule (likewise for b and c).","r":"Balbharati Maths XII Part 1, p.81"},{"x":"cos(A/2) = sqrt(s*(s - a)/(b*c))","n":"Half-angle formula; s = (a + b + c)/2. Also sin(A/2) = sqrt((s - b)(s - c)/(b c)).","r":"Balbharati Maths XII Part 1, p.82"},{"x":"A = sqrt(s*(s - a)*(s - b)*(s - c))","n":"Heron's formula for the area of the triangle.","r":"Balbharati Maths XII Part 1, p.84"},{"x":"tan((B - C)/2) = ((b - c)/(b + c))*cot(A/2)","n":"Napier's analogy.","r":"Balbharati Maths XII Part 1, p.84"}],"w":["The cosine rule gives an angle from three sides; the sine rule needs a side-angle pair."]},"3.3":{"s":"Trigonometric functions are not one-one over their whole domain, so their inverses are defined on a chosen interval: the principal value branch. For example sin⁻¹ takes values in [−π/2, π/2], cos⁻¹ in [0, π] and tan⁻¹ in (−π/2, π/2). The chapter gives the domains and ranges, the rules for negative arguments, and addition formulas such as the one for tan⁻¹x + tan⁻¹y.","f":[{"x":"sin^-1(x) + cos^-1(x) = pi/2","n":"For -1 <= x <= 1; also tan^-1(x) + cot^-1(x) = pi/2 for all real x.","r":"Balbharati Maths XII Part 1, p.96"},{"x":"sin^-1(-x) = -sin^-1(x)","n":"Odd functions: also tan^-1(-x) = -tan^-1(x).","r":"Balbharati Maths XII Part 1, p.96"},{"x":"cos^-1(-x) = pi - cos^-1(x)","n":"For -1 <= x <= 1; likewise cot^-1(-x) = pi - cot^-1(x).","r":"Balbharati Maths XII Part 1, p.96"},{"x":"tan^-1(x) + tan^-1(y) = tan^-1((x + y)/(1 - x*y))","n":"For x > 0, y > 0 and xy < 1; if xy > 1 add pi.","r":"Balbharati Maths XII Part 1, p.96"},{"x":"tan^-1(x) - tan^-1(y) = tan^-1((x - y)/(1 + x*y))","n":"For x > 0, y > 0.","r":"Balbharati Maths XII Part 1, p.97"}],"w":["sin⁻¹(sin 5π/6) is π/6, not 5π/6: the answer must lie in the principal range.","sin⁻¹x is the inverse function, not 1/sin x."]}};
